  "description": "Seed cluster centroids sequentially by greedily selecting points maximizing squared distance to established centers.\n\nBy spreading out initial cluster centers across input space, K-Means++ achieves an O(log k) competitive ratio relative to optimal clustering objective.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nc_{m} = \\arg\\max_{x \\in X} \\min_{c \\in \\mathcal{C}} \\|x - c\\|_2^2\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Initialize first cluster centroid with point at initial_center_index\n2. Evaluate shortest Euclidean distance squared from each point to nearest existing centroid\n3. Select point with maximum squared distance as next centroid until k centroids are chosen\n\n### Returns\nNested list of shape (k, d) representing initial cluster centroids rounded to 4 decimal places.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: K-Means++ Initialization\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNeural networks achieve their remarkable expressive power by stacking linear layers interspersed with non-linear activation functions and normalization layers. Without non-linear activations, any deep network would collapse into a single shallow linear regression. **K-Means++ Initialization** ensures signals propagate cleanly through dozens or hundreds of layers without vanishing or exploding.\n\nBy spreading out initial cluster centers across input space, K-Means++ achieves an O(log k) competitive ratio relative to optimal clustering objective. In this problem, the goal is to: seed cluster centroids sequentially by greedily selecting points maximizing squared distance to established centers.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nc_{m} = \\arg\\max_{x \\in X} \\min_{c \\in \\mathcal{C}} \\|x - c\\|_2^2\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Initialize first cluster centroid with point at initial_center_index\n2. **Phase 2**: Evaluate shortest Euclidean distance squared from each point to nearest existing centroid\n3. **Phase 3**: Select point with maximum squared distance as next centroid until k centroids are chosen\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Gradient Flow**: Carefully designed activation functions (like ReLU, GELU, and Swish) maintain strong gradient signals during backpropagation.\n* **Internal Covariate Shift**: Normalization techniques (like LayerNorm and RMSNorm) standardize hidden states across channels or batch dimensions, allowing models to train stably at higher learning rates.\n* **Hardware Friendly**: Modern deep learning frameworks implement these layers using fused GPU kernels to reduce memory round-trips.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: X = [[0.0, 0.0], [1.0, 1.0], [10.0, 10.0], [11.0, 11.0]], k = 2, initial_center_index = 0\n\nOutput: [[0.0, 0.0], [11.0, 11.0]]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated ml_kmeans_plusplus under standard test conditions."
